// Selenium Manager — Rust binary crate.
//
// Selenium's `rust/Cargo.toml` declares a single binary crate
// (`selenium-manager`) with a deep dependency tree (clap, reqwest,
// tokio, zip, …). Upstream Bazel `rust_binary` rule wraps a
// `cargo`-shaped build via `rules_rust`.
//
// The aeb conversion uses the upstream Cargo.toml unchanged —
// `rust.cargo_project_existing(b)` runs `cargo build --release`
// from this dir without regenerating Cargo.toml. The
// `binary_name("selenium-manager")` setter is the artifact-write
// hint; downstream consumers (e.g., the Python wheel that vendors
// selenium-manager into the wheel via setuptools-rust) can
// `build.dep` on this file and read the `cargo_binary` artifact
// for the path.
//
// **Not converted in this pass:** the Bazel-side `rust_test` rules
// in `tests/BUILD.bazel`. `rust.cargo_test(b)` or equivalent would
// run `cargo test`; not present in lib/rust today. The shape gap
// is small enough that adding it is mechanical (new builder, pure
// `cargo_test_cmd` helper, parallel `_existing` variant).
//
// End-to-end execution needs a working Rust toolchain on PATH plus
// network access for cargo's transitive crate fetch (the lockfile
// is pinned; cargo just verifies). Roughly 5-10 minutes clean
// build on a recent dev box.

import build
import rust
import rust (binary_name)

main() {
    b = build.start()
    rust.cargo_project_existing(b) {
        binary_name("selenium-manager")
    }
}
